1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,030
Ask your computer what a
tenth plus two tenths is.

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,527
Zero point three, fifteen
zeros, and a four.

3
00:00:05,787 --> 00:00:08,507
Most software keeps numbers in binary
with a fixed number of digits,

4
00:00:08,557 --> 00:00:11,602
and a tenth doesn't fit. So it rounds.

5
00:00:11,872 --> 00:00:14,292
Store time that way, and the
further from 1970 you go,

6
00:00:14,342 --> 00:00:17,022
the blurrier it gets. At
the dinosaurs' extinction,

7
00:00:17,072 --> 00:00:19,292
a quarter of a second.
When the Earth formed,

8
00:00:19,342 --> 00:00:20,267
sixteen.

9
00:00:20,537 --> 00:00:23,377
OpenChronology stores every
time as an exact fraction.

10
00:00:23,427 --> 00:00:27,347
A tenth is one over ten. A third
of a second stays exactly a third,

11
00:00:27,397 --> 00:00:30,136
four billion years back or
a billion times zoomed in.

12
00:00:30,216 --> 00:00:32,694
It's open source, so
check the math yourself.
